Z||000006||ChadMan||05-22-2001||09:01 AM||||Drew, in addition to all the good points you've made here, electric cars (like the GM EV1) need new batteries every 5-7 years, at a cost of around $5000.  And what will all those disposed batteries do to our precious environment?<P>Small, fuel-efficient gasoline-burning automobiles are the answer (for now)...but no one wants them!<P>I hate every single hypocrite who babbles on about gas prices and/or the environment, but drives a gas-guzzling SUV, van, full-size truck or luxury sedan.<P>I saw a "Recycle - One Can Saves Toucans" bumper sticker this morning on a 15-mpg Dodge Durango.  I honestly believe that man's life has no value whatsoever.<P>Yes, I have issues.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||05-22-2001||09:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>...electric cars (like the GM EV1) need new batteries every 5-7 years, at a cost of around $5000 </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not sure about the EV1, but many of the folks here were leasing electric powered Ford Rangers. As I understand it, it was fairly common for the batteries to fail before the 3-year lease was up. People were only getting 40-50 miles from a charge.<BR>We still have a way to go with battery technology.<P>As for gas prices?<BR>Let the market control the prices. Not the government. When it gets too expensive, people will park the land yachts and drive sensibly sized cars.  <BR>Most every time the government puts its nose into something, it tends to make the situation worse. (Witness the requirement for 40 or so different "blends" of gas, depending upon where you live. Spreads the refineries capacities very thin.)<BR>Aside from that, I'm already hearing rumors that gas prices are expected to <I>drop</I> after the US Memorial Day.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJoe||05-22-2001||09:57 AM||||I too think that electric cars have very little future.  Less than 100 miles a charge???  So, if you ever need to go on a road trip, you have to find somewhere to charge up before you run out of juice.  And those charges take overnight!  Just doesnt seem right to me.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
